</TabItem>
   <TabItem Header="About" Style="{StaticResource NeonTabItem}">
<ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
<Border Style="{StaticResource Panel}" Margin="0,16,0,0">
<StackPanel>
<TextBlock Text="About VidShrink" Style="{StaticResource H2}"/>
<TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Body}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,12,0,0" Text="VidShrink is an offline Windows tool for target-size video compression and format conversion. Give it a file and a size ceiling; it works out the settings that lose the least of what a person can actually see, and never returns a file larger than you asked for."/>
<TextBlock Text="How The Engine Thinks" Style="{StaticResource H2}" Margin="0,20,0,8"/>
<TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Body}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="Most size-target compressors apply a fixed table: this many megabytes per minute becomes that resolution. That table is wrong for every clip that is not average. VidShrink measures instead. It encodes short samples of your actual file at two resolutions and reads how many bits it really costs, and how much of that cost disappears when the picture is scaled down. Those two measurements make the plan specific to this clip rather than to video in general."/>
<TextBlock Text="Measured, Not Assumed" Style="{StaticResource H2}" Margin="0,20,0,8"/>
<TextBlock FontFamily="{StaticResource FontMono}" Foreground="{StaticResource TextBody}" Text="complexity      measured from your file, not its bitrate&#x0a;detail falloff  measured; decides if scaling down is worth it&#x0a;resolution      continuous search, not a fixed ladder&#x0a;frame rate      searched alongside resolution, not after it&#x0a;codec           chosen from how hard the target actually is&#x0a;audio           share of budget shrinks as the target tightens&#x0a;estimate        a measured number with a range, shown up front"/>
<TextBlock Text="Where The Loss Goes" Style="{StaticResource H2}" Margin="0,20,0,8"/>
<TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Body}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="Below a certain bit budget something has to give. The engine spends the loss where the eye is least sensitive: softness before blocking, fewer pixels before broken pixels, mono audio before a starved picture. It also knows when to stop — once quality reaches the point where more bits buy nothing you could see, it hands back a smaller file instead of padding it to the target."/>
<TextBlock Text="Scenario Awareness" Style="{StaticResource H2}" Margin="0,20,0,8"/>
<TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Body}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="A 1.2x reduction and a 600x reduction are not the same problem, so they do not get the same treatment. Light targets keep everything and simply spend the budget. Balanced targets allow scaling. Aggressive and extreme targets unlock frame-rate reduction, move to H.265, and cut the audio share — and the app tells you what it changed and why, in plain language, before you press start."/>
<TextBlock Text="AI Mode" Style="{StaticResource H2}" Margin="0,20,0,8"/>
<TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Body}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="AI mode only creates a prompt and validates pasted JSON. No AI is embedded, so VidShrink works offline, asks for no API key, and falls back to the complete automatic engine when an answer is malformed or incompatible."/>
<TextBlock Text="Codecs" Style="{StaticResource H2}" Margin="0,20,0,8"/>
<TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Body}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="H.264 offers the broadest compatibility. H.265 compresses more efficiently. VP9 is a strong WebM choice. AV1 maximizes modern compression at higher CPU cost. Stream copy is fastest and lossless when the destination supports the source streams."/>
<TextBlock Text="System Status" Style="{StaticResource H2}" Margin="0,20,0,8"/>
<TextBlock x:Name="TxtSystemStatus" Style="{StaticResource MonoValue}" TextWrapping="Wrap"/>
</StackPanel>
</Border>
</ScrollViewer>
</TabItem>
  </TabControl>
<TextBlock x:Name="TxtStatusBar" Visibility="Collapsed"/>
</Grid>
 </Border>
</Window>

